6.85.m:
It is taught as reaching to the feet, to the knees, or equal to the navel; / the wise man should have the sacred thread made superior, intermediate, or inferior.

Likewise, measured by the days of the year, or by half of that, or a quarter, / one should fashion it with the thread, having one hundred and eight knots,

or, O Pārvatī, endowed with half that number. / On a liṅga it is called 'liṅga', accompanied by Gaṅgā and snakes;

on an image, O goddess, the sacred thread is called a 'forest-garland' (vanamālā). / It should be made according to what looks beautiful, whereby Viṣṇu is pleased.

One should always have one excellent sacred thread made, called the fragrant one (gandha); / and that which is woven of thread should be made by Vaiṣṇava men.

The sacred thread dedicated to Viṣṇu is also declared for the gods, / and for devotees such as Ambarīṣa, and others such as Dhruva.

After that, O Pārvatī, the sacred threads are to be offered here. / The first lunar day is declared for the Giver of Wealth (Kubera) for the thread-investment ritual.

The second lunar day belongs to goddess Lakṣmī, the best of lunar days; / the third is declared for you, and the fourth for the Lord of the Gaṇas (Gaṇeśa);

the fifth belongs to the Moon, and the sixth to Kārttikeya; / the seventh is proclaimed for the Sun, and the eighth is taught for Durgā;
